+
+## The sandbox checks switch
+
+The sandbox checks are the owner's yes that this rule needs before each change to a sandbox or scratch org, and the question the guard asks to get it. The owner can turn them off for one session in Claude Code. The agent can then deploy to a sandbox, fix what failed, and deploy again without asking the owner each time.
+
+- When the owner tells you to turn the sandbox checks off, run this command as the whole command line, with nothing before or after it: `node .claude/hooks/guard-protected-orgs.cjs --sandbox-checks off`. The guard asks the owner once to confirm.
+- When the owner tells you to turn the sandbox checks on again, run the same command with `on` in place of `off`.
+- Never run the `off` command unless the owner told you to in this session.
+- The sandbox checks stay off for 12 hours, in that session only. When the 12 hours end, the guard asks again. Run the `off` command again only after the owner tells you to again.
+- While the sandbox checks are off, the owner's instruction to turn them off is the owner's yes for each of these on a sandbox or scratch org:
+  - deploying, writing data, and running `sf apex run`;
+  - deploying a profile;
+  - deleting metadata with a destructive change inside a deploy.
+- That instruction is also the yes that `managing-work-items` asks for before a deploy to a sandbox that other people use.
+- The switch never covers production, an org the guard cannot classify, a DevOps Center pipeline deploy, deleting an org, or `sf project delete source`.
+- The switch changes only what this section lists. Every other rule still applies: run the permission set preflight, record each deploy and each delete with `sf-component-tracker`, as `component-tracker.md` says, and confirm what a change is for before you build it, as `confirm-salesforce-changes.md` says.
+- While the sandbox checks are off, run each `sf` command as its own command line, with its own `-o <sandbox>`. The guard still asks when it cannot tell which org every command on a line goes to and the default org is not a sandbox.
+- Never write, change, or delete the files the guard keeps in the system's temporary folder.
 
